<BaseLayout
  title="Omega-3 Fish Oil: EPA/DHA Dosing & Research — Nawtch"
  description="What trials actually show for omega-3 and heart/brain health, realistic EPA/DHA dosing, and why fish oil pills haven't matched fatty fish in research."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/omega-3/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Omega-3 / Fish Oil</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">EPA and DHA for heart and brain health, with a real evidence gap between fatty fish and fish oil pills.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/">← Back to Supplements</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">Not medical advice — check with a doctor before starting, especially if you take blood thinners or have a bleeding disorder.</p>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        EPA (eicosapentaenoic acid) and DHA (docosahexaenoic acid) are the two long-chain omega-3 fatty acids
        found in fatty fish and fish oil supplements — distinct from the shorter-chain ALA found in plant sources
        like flaxseed, which the body converts to EPA/DHA only inefficiently. EPA and DHA are incorporated into
        cell membranes throughout the body, including the heart and brain, and influence inflammation and
        triglyceride metabolism, which is the basis for most of the cardiovascular research below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        This is a supplement where the evidence is genuinely mixed, and the details of which study used what
        matter a lot.
      </p>
      <strong>Cardiovascular — a real split result:</strong> Bhatt DL, et al. "Cardiovascular Risk Reduction with
      Icosapent Ethyl for Hypertriglyceridemia" (REDUCE-IT), <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2019. In
      statin-treated patients with high triglycerides, 4g/day of a purified, prescription-only EPA formulation
      (icosapent ethyl) cut major cardiovascular events by 25% relative to placebo. It's worth being precise here:
      this trial used a high-dose prescription drug, not an over-the-counter fish oil supplement.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa1812792" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the trial →</a>
      <br /><br />
      Manson JE, et al. "Marine n-3 Fatty Acids and Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease and Cancer" (VITAL), <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2019. In a general-population trial, a more typical 1g/day
      fish oil supplement showed <strong>no significant reduction</strong> in major cardiovascular events overall
      — though a secondary analysis found a benefit specifically in people who ate little fish to begin with.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa1811403" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the trial →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Cognitive function:</strong> evidence here is weaker still — several RCTs of DHA-rich fish oil have
      largely failed to improve cognition or slow decline in older adults with normal or mildly impaired
      cognition. If there's a benefit, it looks concentrated in earlier-stage decline, not a general
      brain-boosting effect for healthy adults.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Food vs. supplement — a documented gap:</strong> cohort studies pooling over a million participants
      associate higher fish consumption with meaningfully lower cardiovascular risk, a contrast with VITAL's null
      supplement result above. Fish oil isn't necessarily "the same thing in a pill" as eating fish — the fish
      itself may carry other nutrients or displace less healthy foods in the diet, and at least one large cohort
      has linked habitual fish oil supplement use to a small increase in atrial fibrillation risk in people
      without existing heart disease, which is worth knowing rather than assuming supplements are risk-free.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Typical Dosing</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Per the NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, combined EPA+DHA intake up to 3g/day (including diet) is
        generally recognized as safe; doses above 5g/day aren't generally recommended without medical supervision
        due to a bleeding-risk signal at high intakes. Most OTC fish oil supplements provide 250mg–1,000mg
        combined EPA+DHA per serving — check the label rather than assuming a standard dose.
      </p>
      <br />
      <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Omega3FattyAcids-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH Office of Dietary Supplements — Omega-3 Fatty Acids Fact Sheet →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        High-dose fish oil can increase bleeding risk, so anyone on blood thinners (warfarin, aspirin therapy) or
        with a bleeding disorder should talk to a doctor first. As above, one large cohort linked habitual
        supplement use to a modest increase in atrial fibrillation risk in people without pre-existing heart
        disease — a reason to treat high-dose, long-term use as something to discuss with a doctor rather than a
        default.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Omega3FattyAcids-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH Office of Dietary Supplements — Omega-3 Fatty Acids</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
